-- title:
--   Theorem 4 — finite-sample minimax sandwich $\frac{\mu\,\mathrm{kl}(1-\delta,\delta)}{D}\le V_\delta\le\frac{\mu\{\log(1/\delta)+z_+\}}{D}$, attained by a two-sided SPRT
-- statement:
--   Fix an integer depth $d\ge0$, a survival probability $a\in(0,1]$ with reach probability $p=a^d$, an entry parameter $\kappa\in(0,1]$, a root bonus and margin with $0<\varepsilon<b<\kappa p$, and the law $\nu_0$ of the old transcript. Put
--
--   $$
--   t=\frac b{\kappa p},\quad q_0=\frac{b-\varepsilon}{\kappa p},\quad D=\mathrm{kl}(q_0,t),\quad z_+=\log\frac{1-q_0}{1-t},\quad \mu(a,d)=\frac{1+\sum_{j=0}^d a^j}{a^d}.
--   $$
--
--   Let $V_\delta$ be the infimum, over all $\delta$-sound and $\varepsilon$-complete audit rules (using old data and private randomness, possibly aborting and restarting), of $\sup_{q\le q_0}\mathbb E_qC$, where $C$ is the new physical cost. Then:
--
--   1. for every $0<\delta<\tfrac12$,
--   $$
--   \frac{\mu(a,d)\,\mathrm{kl}(1-\delta,\delta)}{D}\ \le\ V_\delta\ \le\ \frac{\mu(a,d)\{\log(1/\delta)+z_+\}}{D};
--   $$
--   2. for every $0<\delta<\tfrac12$, the upper bound is achieved by a terminating two-sided likelihood-ratio rule: the SPRT that traverses full episodes, accepts when $S_n\ge\log(1/\delta)$ and stops without a certificate when $S_n\le-\log(1/\delta)$ is $\delta$-sound and $\varepsilon$-complete, stops almost surely under every $q\in[0,1]$, and has $\sup_{q\le q_0}\mathbb E_qC\le\mu(a,d)\{\log(1/\delta)+z_+\}/D$;
--   3. $V_\delta/\log(1/\delta)\to\mu(a,d)/D$ as $\delta\downarrow0$.
--
--   The same navigation multiplier $\mu(a,d)$ appears in both bounds, so the bounds pin down the exact physical cost of re-auditing one changed mechanism behind known stochastic navigation, up to the gap between $\mathrm{kl}(1-\delta,\delta)$ and $\log(1/\delta)+z_+$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $V_\delta$ and all expectations are in $[0,\infty]$; the limit uses the real value of $V_\delta$, which is finite by item 1. The SPRT is stated with trivial private randomness, so it is one of the rules over which $V_\delta$ is the infimum.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: Vδ, the infimum over δ-sound ε-complete rules that may use old data and private randomness → for every fixed old-data law ν₀ on W₀: V ν₀ … δ, the infimum over private randomness (U : Type, ρ) and rules on W₀ × U, with the old data's law fixed by the environment; reason: the source does not say whether the old-data law is fixed; stating the theorem for every fixed law is the stronger reading and also gives it for the infimum over laws
--   - reading: "The upper bound is achieved by a terminating two-sided likelihood-ratio rule" → the named rule sprtRule (full traversal, thresholds ±log(1/δ), never stops before the first target observation) is δ-sound, ε-complete, stops almost surely under every q ∈ [0, 1], and has sup_{q ≤ q₀} E_q C ≤ the upper bound; reason: this is the rule the proof constructs; "terminating" is read as almost-sure stopping under every parameter, as the proof shows
--   - encoding: Vδ/ log(1/δ) → µ(a, d)/D as δ ↓ 0 → Tendsto (fun δ => (V ν₀ a d b κ ε δ).toReal / Real.log (1 / δ)) (𝓝[>] 0) (𝓝 (mu a d / D)); reason: V is finite for δ < 1/2 by the upper bound, so the real value is the source's
--   - encoding: navigation and target outcomes with their stated conditional laws given the history; old transcript generated by a common kernel under all compared parameters; private randomness → side variable w ∼ ν (old data and private randomness, law free of q), independent of an i.i.d. Bernoulli(a) navigation tape and an i.i.d. Bernoulli(q) target tape; the k-th navigation attempt reads navigation entry k, the j-th target attempt reads target entry j (lawQ); reason: attempts are predictable, so each reads a fresh entry independent of everything observed; the joint law of (old data, randomness, fresh history) is the source's
--   - encoding: C the new physical cost (root setups, navigation attempts, target attempts), N the number of target observations, expectations E_q → cost and targetCount valued in ℕ∞, expectations as lintegrals in ℝ≥0∞ (expCost, expTargets); reason: same quantities; a rule that never stops has cost ⊤ and never accepts
--   - encoding: an auditor may adaptively abort or restart; root resets allowed, internal resets not → actions setup / attempt / accept / stop; setup issued during an episode abandons it; attempt with no active episode acts as a root setup (cost 1); decisions are measurable in w for each fresh history; reason: every abort/restart is a root setup; no action yields a free observation; measurability makes the acceptance events and costs well defined
--   - encoding: old data and private randomness of any kind → old-data space W₀ : Type and randomness space U : Type (universe 0) with any measurable structure and probability law; reason: a rule depends on (old data, randomness) only through countably many measurable decisions, so pushing it forward to a space in Type (e.g. ℕ → Action-valued decision tables) changes no law, cost or acceptance probability
-- source:
--   Q. Li, When Can Physical Evidence Survive a World-Model Update? Finite-cost re-auditing and reusable policy certificates, review revision of 3 November 2026 (STOC 2027 submission, sha256 a20c437b34115b84982339e9a6fa319cb3a66f66d9d4c474465cbb954c79093d), Theorem 4 (A finite-sample minimax sandwich), p.3 (PDF p.4), display (1) and the two sentences after it

